#### 4.2.7 Event Plane Resolution

Since finite multiplicity limits the angular resolution of the reaction plane reconstruction, the \(v_{n}^{\rm obs}\) has to be corrected for the event plane resolution to obtain the real \(v_{n}\),

\[v_{n} = \frac{v_{n}^{\rm obs}}{\langle\cos n(\Psi_{n}-\Psi_{r})\rangle} \tag{4.25}\]

Figure 4.10: First-order event plane distribution for east, west and full event plane. The shift correction forces the event plane distribution to became flat.

where angle brackets denote an average over a large event sample. The event plane resolution is the denominator of the above equation. For the case of the 1st-order event plane from the BBC, \(m=1\) and the event plane resolution for the \(k\)-th harmonic calculation reduces to \(\langle\cos k(\Psi_{1}-\Psi_{r})\rangle\). The BBC event plane obtained from the detector on one side of the collision (east or west) is called a sub-event plane. We have two independent sub-event planes from the two BBCs. The correlation between these two event plane angles can be expressed as

\[\langle\cos k(\Psi_{\rm east}-\Psi_{\rm west})\rangle =\langle\cos k(\Psi_{\rm east}-\Psi_{r})\rangle\ \ \langle\cos k(\Psi_{\rm west}-\Psi_{r})\rangle \tag{4.26}\]

If we assume that the two sub-event planes have the same resolution, then the

Figure 4.11: The event plane resolution for the \(n\)th \((n=km)\) harmonic of a particle distribution with respect to the \(m\)th harmonic plane, as a function of \(\chi_{m}=v_{m}/\sigma\). This plot is from Ref. [14].

sub-event plane resolution is

\[\langle\cos k(\Psi_{\rm sub}-\Psi_{r})\rangle = \langle\cos k(\Psi_{\rm east}-\Psi_{r})\rangle\] \[= \langle\cos k(\Psi_{\rm west}-\Psi_{r})\rangle\] \[= \sqrt{\langle\cos k(\Psi_{\rm east}-\Psi_{\rm west})\rangle}\]

The term inside the square-root should always be positive, if the sub-events are correlated. However, for small amounts of flow, fluctuations and/or nonflow correlations can cause this term to be negative. A combination of the east and west sub-event plane vectors provides the full event plane. When the sub-event plane resolution is low, we can approximate the full event plane resolution as

\[\langle\cos k(\Psi_{\rm full}-\Psi_{r})\rangle\approx\sqrt{2}\langle\cos k( \Psi_{\rm sub}-\Psi_{r})\rangle \tag{4.28}\]

which can be obtained from the sub-event plane resolution. A more detailed and accurate estimation of the event plane resolution from Ref. [14] is given by

\[\langle\cos km(\Psi_{m}-\Psi_{r})\rangle=\frac{\sqrt{\pi}}{2\sqrt{2}}\chi_{m} \exp(-\chi_{m}^{2}/4)[I_{(k-1)/2}(\chi_{m}^{2}/4)+I_{(k+1)/2}(\chi_{m}^{2}/4)] \tag{4.29}\]

where \(\chi_{m}=v_{m}/\sigma\) and \(I_{\nu}\) is the modified Bessel function of order \(\nu\). This resolution function is plotted in 4.11. In the event plane resolution calculation used in this dissertation, Eq. 4.29 has been used.

