energy are not the same. At 200 GeV and 62.4 GeV, it was previously reported that directed flow is not different within errors for AuAu and CuCu [35]. The present work demonstrates that this behavior extends to lower energies. Directed flow provides information about the collision process that complements the more widely-studied elliptic flow. Elliptic flow is imparted after a number of momentum exchanges among particles, and the number of such exchanges depends on the dimensions of the participant system and on its density. Consequently, for a given collision centrality, elliptic flow varies with the mass of the colliding nuclei. In contrast, the observation that directed flow does not vary with the mass of the colliding nuclei is a reflection of the different mechanism that generates \(v_{1}\): here, the relevant feature is the rapidity shift undergone by particles that are initially located at different distances from the center of the participant volume [74] -- a fundamental characteristic of the relativistic heavy-ion interaction process.

